 ### CUDA Isosurface Extraction with Overlapped Execution
-An optimization for CUDA-based isosurface extraction that overlaps data transfers and kernel executions to hide latency [S2]. It executes three overlapping pairs of operations: 1) calculation of vertices and transfer of triangles from the previous slice pair; 2) calculation of triangles and transfer of vertices; 3) compaction of triangles and transfer of the next slice [S2]. Constant memory is used for the lookup table and isovalue [S2]. Device initialization takes ~4 seconds, negligible for large datasets but significant for small ones (<100 MB) [S2]. For a 2048x2048x6936 dataset (23 GB), 16 GTX480 devices achieved speedups of 752.3x (isovalue 1000) and 1489.7x (isovalue 4000) [S2].
+An optimization for CUDA-based isosurface extraction that overlaps data transfers and kernel executions to hide latency, specifically for devices with compute capability 1.1 or higher where host-device memory copies of blocks $\le$ 64 KB are asynchronous [S2]. It executes three overlapping pairs of operations: 1) calculation of vertices and transfer of triangles found for the previous pair of slices; 2) calculation of triangles and transfer of vertices; 3) compaction of triangles and transfer of the next slice [S2]. Constant memory is used for the lookup table and isovalue to improve performance [S2]. Device initialization takes approximately 4 seconds [S2]. For a 2048x2048x6936 synthetic dataset (23 GB), ParCUDAIso with 16 GTX480 devices achieved a TIso speedup of 752.3x for isovalue 1000 and 1489.7x for isovalue 4000 relative to sequential single-core execution [S2].
 
 ### Finite State Machine for Interpolant Modelling
-A representation of isosurface evolution by sweeping from high to low isovalues, where states are cases and transitions occur when a vertex changes from white (lower than $h$) to black (higher than $h$) [S3]. The FSM is constructed by defining states as cases and adding transitions from each state to each other case with one extra black vertex [S3]. Each cube starts with case 0. For example, case 2 (two edge-adjacent black vertices) cannot sweep to case 7 (no edge-adjacent black vertices) because topology forbids losing edge adjacency [S3]. This is used to model watertight Marching Cubes cases and prove completeness of Nielson's cases [S3].
+A representation of isosurface evolution by sweeping from high to low isovalues. States are cases; transitions occur when a vertex changes from white to black. Used to impose constraints on possible configurations [S3]. It is used to model watertight Marching Cubes cases and to prove completeness of Nielson's cases [S3]. For example, case 2 (two edge-adjacent black vertices) cannot sweep to case 7 (no edge-adjacent black vertices) because the topology forbids losing the edge adjacency [S3].
+
+### Connectivity Table Generation
+A method to build a connectivity table from the Marching Cubes `tri_table`. It iterates through all 256 cases, mapping edge indices from `tri_table` to vertex indices using `_mc_verts`. If an edge index is not found or is 0, it assigns 0 to the connectivity table entry; otherwise, it assigns the found vertex index [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Speed:** The improved MC algorithm (with 24 configurations and midpoint selection) is approximately 30% faster than the standard MC algorithm and 40% faster than the Masala algorithm [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
-*   **Parallelism:** Uses multi-thread parallel processing with 3 thread types for 24 configurations, synchronized via critical regions [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Uses constant memory for lookup tables and isovalue in GPU implementations [S2].
-*   **Accuracy:** Increases topological configurations to 24 to avoid voids [S4, abstract only, unofficial]. Resolves face-saddle ambiguities using the Asymptotic Decider [S3].
-*   **Use Cases:** Medical image 3D reconstruction, where accuracy and speed are critical [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel is processed independently.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + F)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices and $F$ is the number of faces in the output mesh.
+*   **Performance:** On GPU clusters, significant speedups (up to 1489.7x) are achievable with parallel execution and overlapped transfers [S2]. However, device initialization overhead (~4s) can be significant for small datasets (<100 MB) [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, parallelizable, widely supported.
+*   **Limitations:** Potential for topological ambiguities and degenerate triangles in standard configurations. Not suitable for discontinuous data without modification.
 
 ## Background
-Marching Cubes 2 was introduced by Nielson & Hamann in 1991 in the paper "The asymptotic decider: Resolving the ambiguity in marching cubes" [S3]. It was developed to address the face-saddle ambiguities present in the original trilinear interpolant used in the standard Marching Cubes algorithm [S3]. The variant with 24 configurations and midpoint selection was later proposed in a 2020 paper for improved 3D reconstruction speed and void avoidance [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
+Marching Cubes 2 is a variant of the original Marching Cubes algorithm [S1, unofficial]. The original algorithm was introduced by Lorensen and Cline in 1987. Marching Cubes 2 likely refers to a specific implementation or lookup table variant used in modern graphics libraries or research code, such as the one found in the `differentiable_volumetric_rendering` repository [S1, unofficial].
 
